# Cursor / AI-coding-tool rules for dungeons-and-documentdb

## Project context
This is a PyCon 2026 demo project showing OSS DocumentDB
(https://github.com/documentdb/documentdb) -- a MongoDB-compatible document
database built on PostgreSQL. The audience is Python developers who have
never used a document database. Code must be beginner-friendly.

## Database rules
- Use `pymongo` only. Do NOT introduce ODMs (Beanie, MongoEngine, etc.).
- DocumentDB speaks the MongoDB wire protocol -- standard pymongo patterns
  work. Connections require TLS (self-signed in dev):
  `mongodb://USER:PASS@HOST:10260/?tls=true&tlsAllowInvalidCertificates=true`
- Always read connection info from environment via `src.db.get_client()`.
- The default port is 10260 (not 27017).
- Vector indexes use the `cosmosSearch` operator:
  - Index spec: `{"key": {"embedding": "cosmosSearch"}, "cosmosSearchOptions": {...}}`
  - Query stage: `{"$search": {"cosmosSearch": {"vector": [...], "path": "embedding", "k": 5}}}`
- Use `vector-hnsw` with cosine similarity unless told otherwise.

## LLM rules
- Anthropic Claude is preferred. OpenAI is the fallback.
- Never hardcode model IDs deep in agent code -- they live in `src/llm.py`
  and are configurable via env (`ANTHROPIC_MODEL`, `OPENAI_MODEL`).
- Keep prompts in `src/tavern/npc.py` and `src/spellbook/wizard.py`.

## Embeddings
- Use Voyage AI when Anthropic is the provider, OpenAI embeddings otherwise.
- Default dimensions: 1024 (Voyage voyage-3-lite) or 1536 (OpenAI
  text-embedding-3-small). The vector index dimensions must match the model.

## Spell data
- `data/srd_spells.json` is CC-BY-4.0 from the 5e SRD ONLY.
- Do NOT add non-SRD spells (no PHB-exclusive content, no homebrew, no
  third-party content unless it is explicitly OGL/CC).

## Style
- Python 3.11+. Modern syntax (`X | Y` unions, `list[T]`).
- Use `rich` for all CLI output -- no bare `print` calls in user-facing flows.
- Keep functions under ~40 lines. Beginners are reading this.
- Type hints everywhere. Docstrings on public functions.

## Testing
- Tests in `tests/` use `pytest`. Mock the LLM client -- don't make real calls.
- The DB itself can be tested live (it's local in Docker).
